+## 2. Full-Spectrum Evaluations
+
+### R28 · Redis quota economics: rotation bought availability, not quota — `adapt_cleanroom` → Issue #1732
+
+**Internal evidence (live telemetry, 2026-10-28):** the tower's `redis.rotation.status` and `redis.stats` tools
+report the Upstash chain exactly as #1698 states: **primary and secondary at 480,000/500,000 estimated commands
+(soft threshold 480K hit), cooldown until 2026-11-01T00:01Z; tertiary promoted active with 92 commands used;
+quaternary fresh at 0; monthly window, next global reset 2026-11-01; auto-rotation enabled.** The existing
+mitigation set (`REDIS_QUOTA_PREEMPTIVE_ROTATION.md` under `infrastructure/mcp-control-plane/docs/`, the
+rotation rule landed 2026-10-27 per `docs/bridge/bridge-20261027-redis-rotation-rule.md`, and the quota ledger)
+is a **failover** design: it keeps the federation alive *within* the same total 500K-per-account budget. It does
+not raise the budget. If tertiary + quaternary burn 500K before the reset, the outage horizon in #1698 closes.
+
+**External evidence (primary sources, probed 2026-10-28):**
+
+- Upstash official billing docs (live fetch of `upstash.com/docs/redis/overall/billing`): free tier = **500K
+  monthly commands, 250 MB, per database**; PAYG = **$0.20 per 100K commands**; Fixed plans from **$10/mo
+  (250 MB, 50 GB bandwidth)** — the docs explicitly name "worker-heavy setups such as Sidekiq, BullMQ, Celery,
+  and cron jobs" as the Fixed-plan fit. Budgets on PAYG are enforced by **rate-limiting, not surprise billing**.
+- Billing semantics that refute the obvious "just pipeline it" instinct (same page, quoted): *"Pricing is based
+  on the number of commands (requests) executed… one command is one billable request"*; *"each command inside a
+  pipeline or transaction is still billed individually, and MULTI and EXEC are billed as well"* (N+2); *"EVAL…
+  billed as one command, plus one command for every redis.call"* — a script doing GET+SET bills as 3 commands.
+- What **is** free: `AUTH`, `HELLO`, `RESET`, `PING`, `SELECT`, `QUIT`, `INFO`, `CONFIG`, `COMMAND` — the
+  tower's health/polling lane (`redis_ping`, `redis_stats`, `redis_rotation_status`) is already zero-cost.
+- Official REST docs (`/features/restapi`, live fetch): `/pipeline` batches N commands into **one HTTP request**
+  with a JSON-array response — a round-trip and latency win, **not** a command-count win. Non-atomic by default.
+- Escape hatch: **Valkey 9.0.5** (valkey.io/releases, live page) — Linux Foundation, **BSD-3-Clause**
+  (valkey-io/valkey probe: 27,537 stars, accept tier), 9.0 line released 2025-10-21 per the Linux Foundation
+  press release. Self-hosting Valkey on existing Render/VPS capacity converts the per-command budget into a
+  flat ops cost — at the price of losing Upstash's serverless REST surface, durability snapshots, and the
+  multi-account rotation logic that already exists.
+
+- **Pros:** the decision memo ends the recurring "rotation saves us" assumption with vendor-published math; the
+  command-shaping audit has at least one already-proven pattern on `main` (`core/state_store.py`: mirror-served
+  reads = 0 ops, 1 SET/DEL per mutation, bounded 200-key SCAN at boot); `MGET`/`MSET`-shaping of N-call loops is
+  a 1-command-saves-N trade the billing doc itself sanctions; the tier table gives maintainers a dated,
+  prices-in-hand comparison instead of a mid-outage improvisation.
+- **Cons:** PAYG/Fixed spend real money (the free chain exists precisely to avoid that); Valkey self-host adds
+  ops burden (persistence, failover, version churn) that a 4-person federation may not want; command-shaping
+  wins depend on call-site patterns that must be measured, not assumed.
+- **Risks:** billing docs are versioned prose — prices move, so the memo must carry its probe date (2026-10-28)
+  and be re-verified before any purchase decision; MGET on huge key sets can hit the 10 MB request cap; Lua is
+  a *trap* here (billed 1 + internal calls, so it saves round-trips but can *raise* billable commands).
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no migration to Valkey "now" (rejected below — costs outweigh the $10/mo alternative at
+  current scale); no EVAL-based batching as a quota strategy (it bills more); no client rewrite in this lane —
+  the audit is a checklist, the fixes land as separate reviewed WOs; no new dependency.
+- **Adoption path → Issue [#1732](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/issues/1732)** (docs-only:
+  1 decision memo `docs/ops/REDIS_QUOTA_ECONOMICS.md` + audit checklist mapped to `core/cache/redis_manager`
+  call sites; maintainer decides stay-free / PAYG / Fixed / self-host with the table in hand).
